  <data name="TroubleshootProblemsTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Common problems</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_Action_RestartToDrive" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Restart from the drive</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_Action_OpenFirmwareSettings" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Open the UEFI settings</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_Action_OpenReadiness" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Check my PC</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_NotListed_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The drive is not in the boot menu</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_NotListed_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>• Plug the drive in before switching the computer on, directly into the computer (not into a hub), and try another port, a USB 2 one if there is one.
• Turn "Fast Boot" off in the UEFI settings: some computers skip USB drives with it.
• On a computer from before 2012, turn on "Legacy" or "CSM" in the settings.
• Check the drive above, and create it again if it does not match.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_WindowsStarts_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Windows starts as usual</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_WindowsStarts_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>• The boot menu key must be pressed several times right after switching on, before the Windows logo.
• Windows fast startup can skip the menu: choose Restart rather than Shut down, or hold Shift while clicking Shut down.
• Simpler: let Windows restart into its startup options and choose "Use a device".</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_SecureBoot_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>"Secure Boot violation" or "image not authorized"</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_SecureBoot_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>This distribution does not start with Secure Boot. Turn Secure Boot off in the UEFI settings (often under "Security" or "Boot"), save (often F10) and restart. Or choose a distribution that starts with it, such as Ubuntu, Linux Mint or Fedora.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_BlackScreen_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Black screen after choosing Linux</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_BlackScreen_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Common with NVIDIA graphics cards. In the boot menu of the drive, choose the entry that mentions "safe graphics" or "nomodeset" (or press E on the first entry, add nomodeset at the end of the line that starts with "linux", then press F10). Once Linux is installed, install the NVIDIA driver.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_Errors_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Error messages or a freeze while Linux starts</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_Errors_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Errors such as "squashfs error" or "I/O error" come from a badly written or faulty drive. Check the drive above, create it again, and try another drive and another USB port if it happens again.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_NoDisk_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The installer finds no disk</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_NoDisk_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The disk of the computer probably goes through Intel RST ("RAID On", "VMD"). Set the storage mode to "AHCI" in the UEFI settings, but prepare Windows first (start it once in safe mode), or it will no longer start. "Check my PC" tells whether this computer uses this mode.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_NoAlongside_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>"Install alongside Windows" is not offered</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_NoAlongside_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The installer cannot make room next to Windows when BitLocker encrypts the disk, when Windows is not fully shut down (fast startup) or when the disk is too full. Turn BitLocker and fast startup off, free some space, then try again.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_NoWifi_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>No Wi-Fi on Linux</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Trouble_NoWifi_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Some Wi-Fi cards (Broadcom above all) need a driver that is not on the drive. Connect with an Ethernet cable or share your phone's connection over USB, then open the drivers tool of the distribution ("Additional Drivers" or "Driver Manager"). "Check my PC" shows your Wi-Fi card and the saved passwords.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_wifi_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Write down your Wi-Fi password</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_wifi_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Linux will need it to connect. The application can show the passwords of the networks saved on this computer.</value>
  </data>
